    "result_text": "All 39 tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI consolidated owner duplicate detection around a single derived `identityKey` and made it a returned field.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `identityKey` property to the `Owner` schema (`<telephone>|<email or empty>|<householdId>`), so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it.\n2. **`Households.java`** \u2014 added `identityKey(telephone, email, householdId)` to build the key.\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 maps `identityKey` onto every returned owner.\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 replaced the three separate checks (`rejectDuplicateTelephone`, `rejectDuplicateEmail`, `rejectDuplicateHousehold`, plus the `sharesHousehold` gate) with a single `rejectDuplicateIdentity`, and removed the now-unused imports.\n5. **`DuplicateIdentityException.java`** + advice handler \u2014 one 409 for the consolidated check.\n\n**Behavior change:** email-only and household-only rejections are gone. Two members of the same household with different telephones now both succeed (previously blocked unless `sharesHousehold`).\n\n**One important nuance I want to flag.** The spec prose says \"reject only when the *whole* identityKey matches.\" I implemented that first and it passed Cp03/Cp10/Cp11/Cp25 but **failed Cp28**. Cp28 creates a second owner that shares only the telephone (different last name/address \u21d2 different `householdId`) and asserts **409**. A literal whole-key comparison returns 200 there, because the household segments differ. So the executable test suite and the prose conflict: the tests require the collision to trigger on shared telephone regardless of household.\n\nTo satisfy the tests (and the explicit \"make it green\" requirement) while keeping the household-members-allowed behavior the task also demands, the collision compares the **identity-bearing part of the key \u2014 telephone + email \u2014 and deliberately excludes `householdId`** (a household is a shared grouping, so its members are not duplicates). The full `identityKey` (including `householdId`) is still what's returned and what the exception reports. This is the only rule consistent with all of Cp03/Cp10/Cp11/Cp23/Cp25/Cp28 simultaneously; no test in the suite ever creates two owners sharing a telephone and expects success.\n\nAll 39 tests are green.",
